ly: regex like /^import\\s+([^;]*?)\\s+from\\s+/ or handle `import 'x'` (nothing to report). Since strings are blanked, `from 'node:fs'` becomes `from          ` — hmm, then we can't easily tell where clause ends. Better: keep string contents in a separate \"code\" view like check-seams does: `code` view keeps strings intact but blanks comments; `bare` view blanks strings too but keeps newlines. check-seams masks comments in both, and strings only in `bare`.\n\nI'll do that: two views same length: `code` (comments blanked, strings intact) and `bare` (comments and string/template-text blanked, but template expressions kept). Then:\n- Import declarations: find in `code` view? Comments blanked, strings intact, template text intact — could false-match `import` inside template text or string. But at top-level, an `import` keyword inside a string would be `code` text inside quotes... risk of false positive. Better to find in a third view: `skeleton` where comments and strings are blanked but template expressions kept — that's `bare`. In `bare`, string specifier blanked, so `import { a } from 'x'` → `import { a } from    `. That's fine for parsing bindings! We only need the clause between `import` and `from`. So regex on bare: /\\bimport\\s+([\\s\\S]*?)\\bfrom\\b/g and also dynamic import( — distinguish: `import(` shouldn't match (`import\\s*\\(`). And `import.meta` — `import` followed by `.` — the regex requires `\\s+` then clause... `import.meta` has no whitespace, so no match. But careful: `import {` where clause includes `{`. We can restrict to top-level (brace depth 0) import.\n\nSimpler: find all matches of /\\bimport\\b/g in bare; check it's at depth 0 (brace depth 0 at that index) and next non-space char is not `(` and not `.`; then read clause until the `from` keyword (in bare) or until `;`/newline for side-effect import. Since specifier is blanked, side-effect import `import 'node:process'` → bare: `import               ` — clause empty → nothing to report. For `import x from 'y'` → clause `x `. Parse clause:\n  - split on commas at top (inside braces)\n  - `* as ns`\n  - `{ a, b as c }`\n  - default name\nBindings list: each binding name plus its position maybe (for reporting line: report line of the import statement? design says \"<行号>\" per item; for imports presumably line of the binding/import declaration. A-13 says line not constrained (\"line 不限\"). Our tests will use own fixtures; we compare only file/kind/name. So line = line of the binding identifier occurrence, or of import statement. I'll use binding position.\n\n- After collecting bindings, count identifier occurrences in `bare` (template expressions kept; string text blanked; comments blanked) excluding import declarations region.\n\nReference counting: need identifier tokens `[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*`; exclude if preceded by `.` or `?.`. Also should exclude identifiers in the import declaration itself (the declaration's bindings). For each binding name, count occurrences outside its import declaration (any import declaration? Design says \"除 import 声明本身外，该标识符在文件里出现 0 次即报告\" — all import declarations excluded? I'd exclude only the bindings' own occurrences within import declarations. Simpler: exclude all import declaration spans from reference counting, since references inside import statements wouldn't be real references anyway... but `export { a }` counts. Fine.)\n\n- For top-level functions: find `function name` / `async function name` / `function* name` at brace depth 0, not `export function`. Compute the function body span: from the `{` after params to matching `}`. Count occurrences of the name outside (declaration name + body) — i.e., occurrences in the file excluding the name token itself and body span. If 0 → report. Design says \"除声明自身的名字和它自己的函数体（从声明的 `{` 到配对的 `}`）外，该标识符出现 0 次即报告\".\n\nSo I need brace matching on `bare`. Careful: braces in template expressions are kept (good), strings blanked (good), regex blanked (good).\n\nHmm, but `function` decl detection: find /\\bfunction\\b/g in bare; ensure depth 0; preceded (ignoring whitespace) by `async` or `export`? Determine keyword: check the token before. If previous token is `export` → skip entirely. Also `export default function` → prev is `default`, prev-prev `export` → skip. If inside an expression like `const f = function name(){}` at depth 0 — is that a \"top-level function declaration\"? Design says \"花括号深度 0 的 function name、async function name、function* name 声明，且前面没有 export\". A function expression with name at depth 0 would match; but reported only if unused. That's conservative-ish (could under-report/over-report?). Edge case unlikely in lib. But note `console.log(function foo(){})` would... no.\n\nThen name = identifier after `function` (and after optional `*`). If next char is `(` → anonymous, skip.\n\nParams then `{`. Find `{` and match.\n\nNow the - depth-0 detection: I'll compute a prefix array of brace depth over `bare`. Note braces inside template expressions count; fine.\n\nWait: what about `export function` detection — I skip functions not preceded by export. But `export { viaExportList }` case in clean fixture: `function viaExportList() {}` then `export { viaExportList }` — the name appears once outside its body → count 1 → not reported. Good.\n\n`export default` for function: prev token 'default' and before 'export'.\n\nImplementation detail for \"previous significant token\": I'll scan backwards from index skipping whitespace, collecting the previous identifier word or punctuation char.\n\nLet me define tokenizer over `bare`:\n- iterate i; if identifier start: read word; push {type:'ident', value, start}. \n- else if one of punctuation `{ } ( ) [ ] , ; . ? ! & | = : + - * % < > ~ ^` push token with value.\nThen function detection uses token list with depth computed.\n\nSimpler: build tokens array with `depth` (brace depth at token start) and compute:\n- imports: token 'ident' with value 'import' at depth 0.\n- functions: token 'ident' with 'function' at depth 0, previous token check.\n\nLet me write the lexer over `bare` producing tokens; comments/strings already blanked so tokenizing identifiers and punctuation is straightforward. Also numbers can be skipped (digits) — they aren't identifiers.\n\nRegex-literal handling must happen during masking, since a regex can contain `{` `}` quotes etc. So maskSource must handle regex detection per rule 4.2.1.\n\n**Regex detection**: \"正则字面量按前一个有效记号判断：前一个记号缺失，或是 `( , = : [ ! & | ? { } ; + - * % < > ~ ^`，或是关键字 `return typeof case in of delete void throw new else do` 时，`/` 起始一个正则\". So I need to track previous significant token while scanning the source. Masking is a single char loop; I can maintain `prev` = the last significant token (identifier/word, or punctuation char). When encountering `/`:\n- if next is `/` → line comment; if `*` → block comment.\n- else: if isRegexStart(prev) → scan regex: from `/`, until unescaped `/` not inside character class `[...]`; then consume flags; blank all chars (in both code and bare? For `code` view keep strings; regex isn't a string. Blank in both, but keep newlines). Note regex may contain quotes — blanked entirely so no confusion.\n- else treat as division (not blanked).\n\nisRegexStart(prev): prev === null, or prev in set of chars, or prev in keyword set.\n\nUpdate prev: for each non-space, non-comment char processed. For identifiers, we need whole word: track word accumulation. Let me implement masking with a small state machine:\n\n```\nlet i=0, prev=null\nwhile i<n:\n  ch=s[i]\n  if whitespace: i++ (leave as is)\n  if ch==='/' && next==='/' → blank to newline; (prev unchanged? design \"前一个有效记号\" — comment isn't a token, so unchanged)\n  if ch==='/' && next==='*' → blank; prev unchanged\n  if ch===\"'\" or '\"' → blank string in bare, copy to code; prev='str' (a value); i after closing\n  if ch==='`' → template handling: blank text part, but keep `${...}` content. For tokenizing we need `${` and `}` preserved. I'll handle by treating template as string but recursing into expressions. Hmm, complexity: template `${ {a:1} }`? Let's handle: on backtick, i++; loop: if `\\` escape → skip 2 (blank); if backtick → end; if `$` and next `{` → we need to keep the `${` and the expression text, then resume template text after matching `}`. Recursively call a function to mask expression? Simplest: when we see `${`, increment depth counter and continue copying characters normally (i.e., treat as code) until the matching `}` at depth 0, then continue template-text mode. So inside expression, normal processing applies (strings inside expressions, etc.). That's more intricate inside a single loop but doable with a mode stack.\n\nGiven time, maybe simpler: implement masking as a function that processes a region with a mode stack:\n\nState: stack of modes: 'code' and 'template'. \n- code mode: normal char handling; backtick → push 'template', i++.\n- template mode: chars are text → blank in bare, copy in code? (Design: template text part blanked for both? For `code` we want to keep string text like check-seams? Our `code` view isn't strictly needed. Actually we only need `bare` for tokenizing, and for import clause detection. Binding names in import clause are in bare. So `code` view may be unnecessary entirely! We don't need string contents anywhere. We can just produce `bare`.\n\nYes — drop `code`. Only `bare` needed. Then template text → blank. `${` → push 'code' with a pending brace depth counter (as pseudo code mode); when in code mode inside template and we see `}` at depth 0 of that expression → pop back to template mode.\n\nLet me implement with explicit stack of frames: {mode:'code'|'template', exprDepth:number}. Actually:\n\n```\nconst modes = ['code']  // stack\nlet exprDepth = 0 // for current code frame if it's a template expression\n```\n\nHmm. Alternative: track with a counter `templateExprDepth`. Let me write:\n\n```\nconst stack = [{ kind: 'code' }]\n```\n\nLoop:\n```\nconst top = stack[stack.length-1]\nif (top.kind === 'template') {\n  ch 处理:\n   '\\\\' → blank both chars (or keep newline)\n   '`' → pop; i++\n   '$' && next '{' → keep `${` (not blanked), push {kind:'code', expr:true}, i+=2\n   else → blank (unless newline), i++\n  continue\n}\n// code\n...\n'`' → push {kind:'template'}, i++\n'{' → if top.expr: top.depth=(top.depth||0)+1; keep; i++\n'}' → if top.expr && (top.depth||0)===0 → pop (back to template), keep '}', i++\n      else if top.expr → top.depth--; keep; i++  (also handle plain code braces not in expr — they're just code, no need track)\n```\n\nFor non-expr code frames, we don't need to track braces for masking (only expose structure for tokenizing depth). But brace depth used for tokenizing: brace matching is done on the final bare string separately, tracking all `{`/`}`. Since template expr braces are kept, depth tracking remains consistent (they are real braces of expressions). Actually `${{a:1}}` in template: `{` from `${` is kept too! The `${` is kept as `$` and `{`... I planned to keep `${` both chars. That adds an extra `{` without matching `}` — because the matching `}` is the expression terminator which we keep as `}`. Yes it matches: `${ ... }` → `{` ... `}` balanced. Good; brace matching on bare stays consistent.\n\nSo keep `${` and the closing `}`. Then brace depth works.\n\nContinue code mode:\n- '/' + '/' → line comment blank\n- '/' + '*' → block comment blank\n- '/' regex start → scan regex, blank\n- quote → scan string, blank (keep newlines? strings can't contain raw newlines except template; for safety keep newlines)\n- identifier → read word, record chars, set prev=word\n- other non-space → prev=ch (but for multi-char operators like `?.`, `=>`, still ok; prev '?' or '.')\n- digits → set prev='num'? For regex detection after a number, `/` is division. Set prev to the digit char, which is not in the regex-start set → division. Good.\n- `)` → prev=')' → division. Good.\n\nAt end return bare string (same length).\n\nNow tokenizing bare:\n- tokens: identifiers `[A-Za-z_$][\\w$]*` plus punctuation chars individually. Compute brace depth per index (depth before char). For `}` decrement first.\n\nWe need per-token depth: depth at token start counting `{` as +1 after, `}` as -1 before. Let's compute `depthAt(i)` array? Cheap enough: build array of depth per char (size n) by scanning; for `{` set depth for that char = current, then current++; for `}` current--, depth = current. Others depth = current.\n\nThen tokens with depth.\n\nImport detection: tokens where value === 'import' and type ident and depth 0. Look ahead: next token; if next value === '(' or '.' or next is nothing → skip. If next value === '{' or '*' or ident → parse clause until token 'from' (ident) at depth 0. All tokens between import and from are bindings clause:\n  - if token '{': iterate until matching '}': names are idents; if ident followed by 'as' then binding is the ident after 'as'; skip 'as' and next. Also skip commas.\n  - if ident 'import' then '*' 'as' ident → binding = last ident.\n  - if first token is ident (default import) → binding = it (note: `import x, { a }` → x then comma then brace).\n  Collect binding tokens with their positions.\n\nThen references: count occurrences of identifier tokens in whole file with same name, excluding:\n  - token positions inside any import declaration span (from import token to the token after 'from' clause? The specifier string is blanked so no tokens there. The declaration span ends at the clause end. For reporting purposes, I'll mark the import statement token span: from 'import' to the last clause token (i.e., token before the blanked string). Actually since specifier is blanked, tokens after 'from' are none until ';' or newline. So import span = from import token index to end of clause (last token consumed).\n  - For function names: exclude the name token and body span.\n\nReference rule: identifier token NOT preceded by '.' . Preceded by '.' means the previous non-whitespace char is '.' (including `?.`). We can check char at index-1 skipping whitespace? Design: \"前一个字符是 `.`（含 `?.`）的不计\". I'll check: scan backwards over whitespace from token start; if char is '.' → skip. (For `?.foo`, char before is '.').\n\nAlso object key `{ a: 1 }` counted as reference (conservative).\n\nAlso keywords like `function`, `if` are identifiers but they only matter if name matches a binding — unlikely.\n\nCount references for binding name = number of ident tokens with that value, not excluded. If 0 → report unused-import at binding token line.\n\nFunction unused: for each top-level function decl (name token, body open/close braces):\n  count ident tokens with same value, excluding the declaration name token and tokens inside body span [openBrace, closeBrace] inclusive, and excluding... the declaration's own name only. If 0 → report unused-function at name token line? Design text output `<path>:<行号> unused-function <name>`; line number of function declaration presumably. A-13 doesn't constrain line. Use name token line.\n\nWait: for a self-recursive function the recursion is inside body which is excluded → 0 outside → report. Good.\n\n`export function` skip: previous token (before 'function' or before 'async') is 'export' or 'default' preceded by 'export'. Note in masked bare, `export` and `function` are idents.\n\nAlso skip `function` when it's a method? e.g. `async function` handled. Also `function` as object property value `foo: function()` anonymous → next token '(' → skip.\n\nBrace matching for function body: find first '{' token after the name/params at depth ... params may contain braces in destructuring `function f({a})`. So find '{' after the params parentheses: track paren depth from '(' after name; find matching ')'; then next token should be '{'; then match braces by depth.\n\nEdge: `function deadGen()` generator: tokens: function, deadGen, '(', ')', '{'. With `function*` the '*' is a token; fine.\n\nNow scanPaths(paths, {root}) → { files, items }.\n- paths: array of relative paths (relative to root). Default handled in main: ['lib'].\n- For each path: resolve path.join(root, p); stat; if not exists → throw usage error? scanPaths is exported; main catches. Design: scanPaths returns {files, items}. For usage errors in main, I'll check existence before calling or let scanPaths throw an Error with code 'ENOENT'; main handles → exit 2.\n- Directories recursive collect .mjs/.js/.cjs; files accepted if extension matches? A-17 scans `scripts/check-unused.mjs` → file. If a file with other ext given? Probably accept any explicitly given file? Design: \"目录递归，只取 .mjs、.js、.cjs\" — applies to dirs. For explicit file, include it. A-17 is .mjs anyway.\n- Deduplicate by resolved path, sort by posix relative path code point.\n- files = count of unique files scanned.\n- items: for each file, scanSource → items with file = posix relative to root, kind, line, name. Sort by file, line, name.\n\nJSON output ordering: \"items 按 file、line、name 排序\" — sort by file then line then name (not kind). Good.\n\nText output sorting: presumably same order. A-14 exact single line. Fine.\n\nUsage errors: unknown flag (any arg starting with '-' that isn't --root/--json), or path missing → stderr message + usage, exit 2. Also --root without value → 2.\n\nNote A-15: `node scripts/check-unused.mjs --no-such-flag` — stdout must not contain `check-unused: ok`. We write usage to stderr only.\n\nNow `check-fast` runs every check-*.mjs with no args, and expects exit 0. check-unused with no args scans lib → must be 0.\n\nAlso A-22: check-fast output must contain `check-unused: ok`.\n\n**Critical**: check-fast runs `check-no-collab`, `check-seams`, etc. Also `check-contract-doc`, `check-frozen-files`, `check-test-discovery`, `check-ci`, `check-write-scope` (run with args? no args → just manifest check).\n\n### Fixtures\n\ntest/fixtures/unused/clean/ok.mjs — must report 0. Requirements:\n- all imports used\n- an import used only in template `${…}`\n- a function used only in object shorthand `{ helper }`\n- a function used before declaration (hoisting)\n- `function viaExportList() {}` + `export { viaExportList }`\n- an `export function`\n- side-effect import `import 'node:process'`\n- a regex literal with quotes `/[\"']/` after which scanning works\n- comments and strings mentioning names don't affect results\n\nDesign says \"名字、数量和每条的应报告/不应报告必须保持\" — so the fixture must contain these elements. I'll construct:\n\n```js\nimport { join } from 'node:path'\nimport { sep } from 'node:path'\n\nconst label = `${join('a', 'b')}`\n\nfunction helper() { return sep }\n\nconst object = { helper }\n\nconst early = later()\n\nfunction later() { return 1 }\n\nfunction viaExportList() { return /[\"']/.test('\"') }\n\nexport { viaExportList }\n\nexport function exported() { return object }\n\nfunction unusedMentionedOnlyInText() { return 0 }\n```\n\nHmm, careful: everything must be used or exported. Let me design clean/ok.mjs carefully so scanner reports 0 with my rules (and ideally with design's intent). Unused things would be reported. Also `early` variable unused doesn't matter (we only check imports and top-level functions). But a top-level function must be used somewhere.\n\nAlso note: scanning includes identifiers in `export function exported` → skip.\n\nLet me write clean/ok.mjs:\n\n```js\n// All imports and functions below are used; comments and strings mention deadName but that is text.\nimport { join } from 'node:path'\nimport 'node:process'\n\nconst template = `${join('clean', 'fixture')}`\nconst quoted = 'escaped \"quote\" and deadName'\n\nfunction usedByShorthand() {\n  return /[\"']/.test(quotedRegexSource) ? 1 : 0\n}\n```\n\nHmm, need regex with quotes `/[\"']/` — after that regex, the rest of the file must still be scanned correctly. So place it mid-file with following statements.\n\nLet me simplify and be explicit:\n\n```js\n// clean/ok.mjs — the scanner must report nothing here.\nimport { join } from 'node:path'\nimport 'node:process'\n\nconst PATH = `${join('clean', 'ok')}`\n\nfunction helper() {\n  return PATH\n}\n\nconst shorthand = { helper }\n\nfunction hoisted() {\n  return shorthand.href\n}\n\nconst early = later()\n\nfunction later() {\n  return early\n}\n\nfunction usesRegex() {\n  return /[\"']/.test('text with deadName')\n}\n\nfunction viaExportList() {\n  return usesRegex()\n}\n\nexport { viaExportList }\n\nexport function exported() {\n  return hoisted\n}\n```\n\nCheck references:\n- join: used in template `${join(...)}` → counted (template expr kept). Good.\n- side-effect import: no binding.\n- helper: used in `{ helper }` shorthand → counted.\n- hoisted: used in exported → counted.\n- later: used in `const early = later()` → counted.\n- usesRegex: used in viaExportList → counted.\n- viaExportList: used in export { viaExportList } → counted.\n- exported: skipped (export function).\n- What about `shorthand.href` — identifier `shorthand` counted (preceded by space); `href` preceded by '.' ignored; fine.\n- `early` used inside later body → irrelevant.\n\nRegex `/[\"']/` inside usesRegex: masking must skip it; strings in there `'\"'` stay as string.\n\nAlso comments mention `deadName` and a string contains `deadName` → not counted (no binding anyway). Design says \"注释和字符串里提到的名字不影响结果\" — need a scenario where a *binding* name appears only in comment/string. Add an import used only in a comment? No—that import would be unused and reported. Hmm; the requirement says comments and strings mentioning names shouldn't *affect* results, i.e., shouldn't create false references. To test that, we need a name that IS reported... but clean must report 0. So instead this behavior is tested in scanSource unit tests. For the fixture, I can include a comment mentioning `join` plus real use — not meaningful. Fine, include comments mentioning names that are used anyway.\n\nActually better: keep clean fixture with \"comment mentions deadName / `import` keyword inside string\" to prove scanning doesn't break. E.g. a string `'import fs from \"node:fs\"'`. That's fine.\n\ndirty/imports.mjs — must report exactly 4 imports:\n- `fs` default import unused\n- `readFileSync` unused, `existsSync` used\n- `joinPath` alias unused (`join` only in comment)\n- `os` namespace import mentioned only in comment and string\n\n```js\nimport fs from 'node:fs'\nimport { readFileSync, existsSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport { join as joinPath } from 'node:path'\nimport * as os from 'node:os'\n\n// join is mentioned here but never used.\nconst note = 'os.platform() and fs exist only in this string'\n\nexport function probe() {\n  return existsSync('.')\n}\n```\n\nCheck `joinPath`: alias binding joinPath unused; `join` appears only in comment → fine (also join not a binding). `os` appears in comment and string → not counted. `fs` appears... in the string only → not counted → report. Good. `readFileSync` unused → report. `existsSync` used → not reported.\n\nBut careful: export function probe → skipped. No unused top-level functions besides probe (exported). We must ensure dirty/imports reports only imports (4).\n\ndirty/functions.mjs — report deadHelper, deadAsync, deadGen, deadRecursive; liveHelper and export function exportedUnused not reported.\n\n```js\nfunction deadHelper() {\n  return 1\n}\n\nasync function deadAsync() {\n  return 2\n}\n\nfunction* deadGen() {\n  yield 3\n}\n\nfunction deadRecursive(n) {\n  return deadRecursive(n - 1)\n}\n\nfunction liveHelper() {\n  return 4\n}\n\nexport function exportedUnused() {\n  return liveHelper()\n}\n```\n\nCheck: deadHelper 0 outside body → report. deadAsync → report. deadGen → report. deadRecursive only self-recursion in body → report. liveHelper used in exportedUnused → not reported. exportedUnused skipped. \n\nAny imports? none. Good.\n\nexpected.json:\n```json\n{\n  \"items\": [\n    { \"file\": \"dirty/functions.mjs\", \"kind\": \"function\", \"name\": \"deadAsync\" },\n    { \"file\": \"dirty/functions.mjs\", \"ki